{\pard\fs26\b\sb360\sa160 4. Architecture and request flow\par}{\pard\fs24\sa140\sl276\slmult1 The browser dashboard calls authenticated Next.js API routes. The server obtains identity from Supabase Auth, validates and extracts uploaded documents, stores resume objects in a private bucket with a user-prefixed path, and writes metadata to PostgreSQL. Analysis requests verify that the resume belongs to the authenticated owner, run the local deterministic TypeScript analyzer, and persist JSON results transactionally. Row Level Security policies scope rows to auth.uid(); the PostgreSQL client enforces TLS certificate verification.\par}{\pard\fs20\i\qc\sa200 Figure 1. Source-derived overall architecture. Editable Mermaid and SVG files are in diagrams.\par}{\pard\fs20\i\qc\sa200 Figure 2. Resume upload and extraction checks.\par}{\pard\fs26\b\sb360\sa160 5. Methodology and evidence-based assessment\par}{\pard\fs24\sa140\sl276\slmult1 Job description text is normalized with NFKC and whitespace rules. The parser scans lines for a small explicit alias taxonomy. Recognized terms become requirements; multiword bullet requirements with no supported alias receive stable unmapped IDs and remain uncertain. Resume text is examined by section-aware lines using complete-term boundaries. No match yields missing; terms in a skills list or very short excerpt yield partial; contextual occurrences yield matched. Unmapped requirements remain uncertain because the system has no supported skill mapping.\par}{\pard\fs20\i\qc\sa200 Figure 3. Requirement extraction and evidence classification.\par}{\pard\fs24\sa140\sl276\slmult1 The score is an evidence-coverage index. Required requirements have weight 5; preferred requirements have weight 3. State credits are matched 1.00, partial 0.55, uncertain 0.35, missing 0.00. The weighted sum divided by total possible weight is rounded to an integer percentage. It is not an employer score or probability.\par}{\pard\fs20\i\qc\sa200 Figure 4. Weighted evidence score and hypothetical counterfactual.\par}{\pard\fs26\b\sb360\sa160 6. Proficiency and counterfactual boundaries\par}{\pard\fs24\sa140\sl276\slmult1 Proficiency is a deterministic heuristic: no useful context gives NOT_ENOUGH_EVIDENCE; skills-list/basic exposure generally gives FOUNDATIONAL; one detailed contextual implementation gives INTERMEDIATE; two distinct detailed contexts can give ADVANCED. Confidence labels describe evidence strength and are not statistically calibrated. The output explicitly says this does not establish competence.\par}{\pard\fs24\sa140\sl276\slmult1 Counterfactual logic changes one requirement state to matched and recomputes the same score. The recommendation is a hypothetical arithmetic impact; it does not prove the person can obtain the skill or advise a training path.\par}{\pard\fs26\b\sb360\sa160 7. Research and future-skills methodology\par}{\pard\fs24\sa140\sl276\slmult1 The research module includes a token-overlap baseline and transformations that remove a section, reverse section blocks, or replace selected verbs. These transformations are rudimentary; no claim of semantic paraphrase robustness or validated experimental performance is made. Research run outputs and manuscript revisions are persisted separately.\par}{\pard\fs20\i\qc\sa200 Figure 5. Implemented research workflow.\par}{\pard\fs24\sa140\sl276\slmult1 The separate ml/skill-intelligence module includes job collection and normalization, a canonical taxonomy, annotation/export validation, QLoRA training and evaluation scripts for Qwen/Qwen3-4B-Instruct-2507, and a forecasting pipeline. The current web UI reports forecasting unavailable. Collected data summary records 500 full descriptions across five companies and two sources; dates are missing for 75%, with 125 reliable dates. Training assets include 56 human-reviewed training examples, 29 held-out gold examples, 112 AI-assisted pseudo-labels, and five human-reviewed validation examples. The configured readiness minimum is ten validation records, so the gate is not currently ready. No adapter checkpoint or validated forecast is available.\par}{\pard\fs20\i\qc\sa200 Figure 6. Separate experimental future-skills pipeline and current readiness boundary.\par}{\pard\fs26\b\sb360\sa160 8. Security and privacy controls\par}{\pard\fs24\sa140\sl276\slmult1 API identity is obtained from the server-side Supabase session rather than a client-supplied owner ID. Resume upload size and signature are checked, storage is private and user-prefixed, database reads/writes include owner constraints, RLS policies are configured, and the PostgreSQL TLS client sets rejectUnauthorized=true. These are code-level controls; a comprehensive external security assessment is not claimed.\par}{\pard\fs20\i\qc\sa200 Figure 7. Authenticated persistence and access boundaries.\par}{\pard\fs26\b\sb360\sa160 9. Technology stack\par}{\pard\fs24\sa140\sl276\slmult1 Next.js 16.4.4, React 19, TypeScript 5.7.3, Supabase SSR/Auth and Storage, PostgreSQL via pg, PDF.js, Mammoth, Vitest, ESLint, Python/Pydantic/Transformers/PEFT/bitsandbytes in the separate ML module. Dependency versions are maintained in package manifests and lockfiles. No repository-wide software license has been selected.\par}{\pard\fs26\b\sb360\sa160 10. Test and verification evidence\par}{\pard\fs24\sa140\sl276\slmult1 At documentation preparation, pnpm test passed 49 tests; pnpm lint passed; Python suites covering forecasting and skill-intelligence passed 66 tests. The production build passed with the Webpack builder after the default Turbopack attempt was blocked in the local sandbox by a child-process port bind restriction. These test counts describe this verification run, not general correctness guarantees.\par}{\pard\fs26\b\sb360\sa160 11. Limitations and scope\par}{\pard\fi-360\li720\fs24\sa120\sl276\slmult1 \bullet\tab The matching engine uses a narrow fixed alias taxonomy and deterministic rules, not a contextual language model.\par}{\pard\fi-360\li720\fs24\sa120\sl276\slmult1 \bullet\tab Evidence extraction can miss synonyms, nuanced negation, document layout, and requirements expressed outside recognized patterns.\par}{\pard\fi-360\li720\fs24\sa120\sl276\slmult1 \bullet\tab Score and proficiency labels are heuristics, not calibrated measures, verified skills, hiring outcomes, or fairness guarantees.\par}{\pard\fi-360\li720\fs24\sa120\sl276\slmult1 \bullet\tab Research transforms do not establish broad semantic robustness.\par}{\pard\fi-360\li720\fs24\sa120\sl276\slmult1 \bullet\tab Future-skill prediction is experimental, not integrated into operational analysis, and not validated for forecast accuracy.\par}{\pard\fi-360\li720\fs24\sa120\sl276\slmult1 \bullet\tab No trained adapter is available; AI-assisted labels remain pseudo-labels and must not be treated as ground truth.\par}{\pard\fs26\b\sb360\sa160 12. Conclusion\par}{\pard\fs24\sa140\sl276\slmult1 The strongest supported contribution is an implemented workflow that combines requirement-level evidence, explicit uncertainty, transparent weighted coverage, and bounded what-if scoring within authenticated, persisted software. The package presents source evidence and limitations so reviewers can evaluate the actual software expression without relying on aspirational claims.\par}}